From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Subject: [PATCH] ESP32 I2C: stop spamming stderr on empty-FIFO poll reads

The ESP-IDF I2C driver polls the FIFO data register in a tight loop as
part of its own transfer-completion wait; every single poll iteration
that lands before new data is pushed hits this empty-FIFO branch. The
model logged it with an unconditional error_report() (a synchronous
fprintf+flush on every call), which is not a QEMU bug or even a real
guest error -- real hardware just returns a stale/undefined byte here,
silently. In one real-firmware boot session, this produced over one
million log lines in a few seconds, each a blocking stderr write,
which throttled the whole TCG main loop badly enough to look like the
emulator had hung (confirmed: the guest's own OLED/HUB75 content kept
genuinely changing throughout, so nothing was logically stuck -- the
dominant stderr write overhead was single-handedly bottlenecking
wall-clock progress).

Switch to qemu_log_mask(LOG_GUEST_ERROR, ...), the standard QEMU idiom
for "guest did something a real device would silently tolerate": by
default (no -d guest_errors passed, which this project's launcher
never passes) it is a no-op, eliminating the overhead entirely while
keeping the diagnostic available for anyone who does pass -d.

--- a/hw/i2c/esp32_i2c.c
+++ b/hw/i2c/esp32_i2c.c
@@ -59,7 +59,7 @@
         return esp32_i2c_get_status_reg(s);
     case A_I2C_FIFO_DATA: {
         if (fifo8_num_used(&s->rx_fifo) == 0) {
-            error_report("esp32_i2c: read I2C FIFO while it is empty");
+            qemu_log_mask(LOG_GUEST_ERROR, "esp32_i2c: read I2C FIFO while it is empty\n");
             return 0xee;
         }
         uint8_t res = fifo8_pop(&s->rx_fifo);
